WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:05.580
Over the course of four decades and 800 episodes,
Saturday Night Live has been among the most

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.720
influential comedy shows in the history of
American TV.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:13.650
It's produced countless catchphrases and iconic
characters; dozens of stars like Bill Murray,

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.570
Amy Poehler, Eddie Murphy, Tina Fey, Will
Ferrell and Leslie Jones — and even one

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:19.910
real-life senator.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:21.450
"Al Franken"

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.800
But what do you really know about the iconic
sketch show?

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.320
Here's the untold truth of SNL.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.810
Johnny Carson forced NBC to create it

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:32.539
In the '70s, Johnny Carson's Tonight Show
was the biggest show on television.

00:00:32.539 --> 00:00:36.680
That gave him all the leverage he needed to
renegotiate his contract, and shorten his

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.070
commitment from five shows a week to four.

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:43.260
That meant that NBC couldn't broadcast Tonight
Show reruns on Saturdays anymore, since they'd

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:44.260
need them for Fridays.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:45.329
The network's solution?

00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:49.060
To create a late night variety show geared
to a young, hip audience.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:53.289
Executives tapped 30-year-old Canadian comedy
writer Lorne Michaels, best known for his

00:00:53.289 --> 00:00:55.469
work on an Emmy-winning Lily Tomlin special.

00:00:55.469 --> 00:01:00.889
By October 1975, the show was ready...though,
not-ready for primetime.

00:01:00.889 --> 00:01:05.060
"And the not ready for prime time players."

00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:06.065
The name game

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.760
"Live from New York, it's Saturday night."

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.359
During the first season, the show was originally
called, simply, NBC's Saturday Night.

00:01:22.359 --> 00:01:24.990
That's because the name "Saturday Night Live"
was already in use.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:29.639
Sportscaster Howard Cosell hosted an ABC primetime
variety show with that name.

00:01:29.639 --> 00:01:33.359
"Lee Majors, he will be with us tonight on
Saturday Night live."

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:37.209
Considering that most people today don't even
remember it existed, it goes without saying

00:01:37.209 --> 00:01:40.109
that ABC's Saturday Night Live wasn't a hit.

00:01:40.109 --> 00:01:43.890
But when that show was canceled, NBC snapped
up the title.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.209
Muppet madness

00:01:45.209 --> 00:01:47.880
In its early years, SNL was a true variety
show.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.359
Jim Henson's Muppets were frequent guests
on the late night circuit in the mid-'70s,

00:01:51.359 --> 00:01:53.439
so it only made sense to include them.

00:01:53.439 --> 00:01:56.679
But we're not talking about Kermit, Fozzie,
and Miss Piggy here.

00:01:56.679 --> 00:02:00.609
Instead, Henson and his crew created new,
monstrous, adult-oriented creatures for a

00:02:00.609 --> 00:02:02.270
segment called "The Land of Gorch."

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:05.779
"What's the matter privy breath?"

00:02:05.779 --> 00:02:11.739
"Scrin, our fair land of Gorch is on the brink
of default."

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:17.920
Unfortunately, the segment tended to grind
each show to a halt, as it wasn't considered

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.010
as fresh as the show's traditional sketches.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.850
The Muppets were gone after the first season.

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:25.360
That's okay, since we got The Muppet Show
in 1976.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:26.790
The audition process

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:31.040
Comedy hotbeds like the GroundLings in Los
Angeles or the Upright Citizens Brigade in

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.150
New York often arrange showcases for top talent
who might be a good fit for the show, and

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:37.870
SNL Talent scouts attend.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:40.750
"So, I just gonna tell you our specials.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:45.709
Uh, we have the, uh, roasted portobello ragu,
it is savory and sweet.

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:51.000
I should tell you uh, the first time I had
this dish I got a little backed up."

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.190
If they like what they see, they invite the
performers to an audition on the SNL stage.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:59.230
Hopefuls have 10 minutes to do stand-up or
present at least two original characters and

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:00.230
celebrity impressions.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:05.700
"It's a bus-a-thon, we're raising money, while
we're on the bus."

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.239
"It's been really cool, like , getting to
hang out with my dad and like, go out on the

00:03:09.239 --> 00:03:10.659
road and stuff like that"

00:03:10.659 --> 00:03:15.519
Among some famous faces who auditioned but
didn't make the cut: Jim Carrey, 13-time host

00:03:15.519 --> 00:03:22.680
John Goodman, Kevin Hart, Stephen Colbert,
Aubrey Plaza, Louis C.K.,Lisa Kudrow, Donald

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.489
Glover, Kel Mitchell, and Zach Galifianakis,
to name just a few.

00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:32.340
"I have a new headshot it's a scratch and
sniff and it smells like failure and onions."

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.689
Entertainment Weekly reports Jennifer Aniston
was offered a slot on the show in the mid-'90s,

00:03:36.689 --> 00:03:38.219
but opted to do Friends instead.

00:03:38.219 --> 00:03:39.469
Probably a smart move.

00:03:39.469 --> 00:03:42.030
Things got weird with Francis Ford Coppola

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:46.780
For decades, each episode of SNL has basically
followed the same format: a bunch of standalone

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:51.110
sketches, a couple of musical performances,
and "Weekend Update."

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:53.990
In 1986, that all went out the window–but
just once.

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:58.019
Acclaimed director Francis Ford Coppola was
the host, and the through-line of the episode

00:03:58.019 --> 00:04:00.599
was that Coppola was "directing" the show.

00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:03.279
"Mr. Coppola I presume."

00:04:03.279 --> 00:04:04.620
"It's Coppola."

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.749
"Don't give me line readings."

00:04:06.749 --> 00:04:09.109
As far as experiments go, it was kind of interesting.

00:04:09.109 --> 00:04:11.040
Too bad it wasn't very funny:

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:16.919
"This pathetic troop of upstart of crows is
the final remnant of live television hanging

00:04:16.919 --> 00:04:18.780
by a thread."

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.360
"It's very sad, it's very sad, isn't it.?

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:22.360
Yup

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:23.820
Short films

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:29.270
Sure, SNL's main draw is the fact that it's
live, but pre-taped segments like fake commercials

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:32.560
and animated shorts have been a show staple
from the beginning.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.000
"The X presidents, George Bush."

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.250
"Read my lips, you're toast."

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:40.550
"Ronald Reagan."

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:42.500
"Just say no.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.289
To pissing me off."

00:04:45.289 --> 00:04:49.639
In the '70s, Albert Brooks rejected Lorne
Michaels' offer to be the show's permanent

00:04:49.639 --> 00:04:52.090
host and instead offered to create short films.

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:55.520
"At best I'd say you have a year to live."

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.560
"I know I overheard you tell doctor Franklin."

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:04.650
"You already knew and you made me go through
the agony of telling you this.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:07.889
You are an inconsiderate and thoughtless woman."

00:05:07.889 --> 00:05:12.629
Mockumentary master Christopher Guest also
contributed short features as a cast member

00:05:12.629 --> 00:05:13.629
in the '80s.

00:05:13.629 --> 00:05:17.660
And The Lonely Island brought more than just
Andy Samberg to SNL: they also brought comic

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:20.200
rap videos like 2005's viral "Lazy Sunday."

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:21.440
"BAKERS DOZEN!"

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.460
"I told you that I'm crazy for these cupcakes,
cousin!"

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:24.740
"Yo where's the movie playin?

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:26.060
"Upper Westside dude"

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:28.420
"Well let's hit up Yahoo Maps to find the
dopest route!"

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:30.210
Legacy

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:35.460
After 40 years of television, it's impossible
to ignore the effect SNL has had on pop culture,

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:37.000
and even America at large.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.320
For one recent example, there's Tina Fey's
show 30 Rock, which was loosely based on her

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:43.789
time as the first female head writer of SNL.

00:05:43.789 --> 00:05:48.400
It ran on NBC for seven seasons, racked up
11 Emmy nominations, and provided us with

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:51.410
some of the most memorable television characters
in recent years.

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:56.430
"Uh, Tracy you do know that Googling yourself
means looking yourself up on the internet."

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:57.430
"I did not know that.

00:05:57.430 --> 00:06:00.560
That explains why Liz Lemon was so cool the
other day."

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:03.840
"Liz Lemon, you mind if I google myself in
your office?"

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:04.840
"Sure Tracy"

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:05.840
"Can I use your computer?"

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:07.970
"How else you gonna do it?"

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:12.930
Not only that, but in 2008, Fey came back
to SNL as a guest to take on the role of Vice

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.110
Presidential nominee Sarah Palin.

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:17.379
"You went to the UN for the first time.

00:06:17.379 --> 00:06:18.860
How was that experience?"

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:21.500
"Oh you know it was just amazing.

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:23.050
So many interesting people.

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:27.860
Though, I have to say I was disheartened by
how many of them were foreigners.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:34.520
I promise that when Senator McCain and I are
elected we are going to get those jobs back

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:36.770
in American hands."

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:40.690
In addition to earning the show some great
ratings, a study after the fact showed that

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:45.099
Fey's impression of Palin actually had an
impact on the candidate's image among Americans,

00:06:45.099 --> 00:06:50.460
and possibly contributed to her and running
mate John McCain's loss of the 2008 election.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:54.000
That's a pretty huge impact for a weekly comedy
show to have, isn't it?

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:55.780
"Oh hey, ya know, sure."

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:56.860
Thanks for watching!

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:59.970
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:03.110
And leave us a comment to tell us about your
favorite SNL sketch...

